  "summary": "A simplified Art Deco style developed for public works projects during the Great Depression.",
  "description": "It features restrained ornamentation and practical design elements for public buildings. This style emerged from the Public Works Administration projects of the 1930s which required economical yet dignified architecture.",
  "culturalContext": "American Art Deco has roots in the style moderne popularized at the 1925 International Exhibition of Modern Decorative and Industrial Arts, Paris, from which the name Art Deco would be drawn retroactively (Exposition internationale des arts décoratifs et industriels modernes). The United States did not officially participate, but Americans—including New York City architect Irwin Chanin and others—visited the exposition, and the government sent a delegation to the expo. Their resulting reports helped spread the style to America. Other influences included German expressionism, the Austrian Secession, Art Nouveau, Cubism, and the ornament of African and Central and South American cultures.",
